ong was Joe Cocker’s version of “With a Little Help from My Friends.” That song is emotionally *part* of the show. The rights were paid for and used for its network TV run and even early DVD releases. But when it came to streaming decades later, the license didn't cover that. The rights for the Joe Cocker master recording were too expensive to re-negotiate for the new medium, so they had to replace it with a cover version. It wasn't a choice—the original permission was expired and didn't apply to streaming. So, it's not that the studio went back on a deal. It's that the deal they originally made was never intended to be a "forever, on any platform, anywhere in the universe" purchase. That kind of blanket license would be astronomically, impossibly expensive. The system of limited, piecemeal permissions is the only way most films can afford to use popular music at all. That's why, while it *feels* like common sense that a paid-for song should be permanent, the reality of copyright law and the music industry means it's a temporary, highly conditional permission—and that's exactly why songs get swapped out.
